I left out the metadata fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `StateHandle<'seen', read>`, which is `aap:caps/state-read`. Adding an `http-client` import would widen this node's authority beyond its declared grant. The request also sends each item's untrusted text into an outbound URL, which lets feed content leave the sandbox and shape a request to an external host. If the platform team approves this, the grant should be changed in the graph definition first, with the host allowlist scoped in the handle. The node can then be updated to match. The implementation below uses only the granted interface.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

